          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Is it ever correct to submit a code root without its decimal characters?",
          "answer": "In ICD-10 coding, a code root can be submitted without its decimal characters in specific situations. The coding structure has three to seven characters. The first character is always a letter, the second is a digit, and the remaining characters can be either letters or digits.\n\nA decimal point is used after the first three characters if additional characters follow. For example, \"I10\" has no decimal, while \"E11.9\" includes a decimal because it has further characters. The first three characters represent the category, and subsequent characters provide more details, such as cause or anatomical site (source: \"ICD-10-CM code structure and the conventions that govern selection\").\n\nSo, while it is acceptable to submit a code root without its decimal characters, it is important to ensure that you are representing the correct level of detail that the diagnosis requires.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
